\strong{Order is bottom to top.} The raster piped in is the \emph{background}; the one
passed as an argument goes \emph{over} it. That matters, because \code{overlay},
\code{dodge}, \code{burn}, \code{hard_light} and \code{subtract} all treat their two inputs
differently. It reads the way a QGIS layer panel does, upwards.

\strong{Nothing is computed until you render.} \code{rvt_blend()} returns a recipe,
not a raster, so a chain of layers costs one pass over the data instead of
one per layer. \code{\link[=rvt_render]{rvt_render()}} writes the file; \code{\link[=rvt_plot]{rvt_plot()}} will draw a
stack directly, so exploring needs no render call at all.
