\section{Choosing a mode}{

Three of the thirteen modes cover most composites. \code{multiply} only ever
darkens, so it is what you want for deepening enclosed ground with a
sky-view factor. \code{screen} only ever lightens. \code{overlay} does both at once -
darkening what is already dark and lightening what is already light - which
adds contrast without washing the image out, and is why \code{\link[=rvt_vat]{rvt_vat()}} uses it
for its openness layer.

The rest are situational: \code{darken}/\code{lighten} take the extreme of the two,
\code{difference} shows where two visualizations disagree, \code{soft_light} is a
gentler \code{overlay}, and \code{dodge}/\code{burn} are aggressive enough to clip. All
thirteen are in \link{rvt_blend_modes}.
}

\section{Ranges}{

Every layer is stretched to 0-1 before blending. Leave \code{range} alone and the
raster's own minimum and maximum are used, measured once before any tile is
read. Set it to concentrate contrast where it matters - \code{c(0.7, 1)} on a
sky-view factor spreads out the band where ordinary terrain actually lives.
\code{\link[=rvt_range]{rvt_range()}} reads a percentile stretch off the raster.
}

\section{Different resolutions}{

Layers may differ in resolution by a whole factor - a 0.2 m orthophoto under
a 1 m hillshade, say - as long as they cover the same extent in the same
coordinate system. The result is rendered at the finest resolution in the
stack, and coarser layers are upsampled onto it with bilinear interpolation
when rendering. Rasters fetched for one place with \code{\link[=rvt_data_lgln]{rvt_data_lgln()}} or
\code{\link[=rvt_data_mapterhorn]{rvt_data_mapterhorn()}} line up this way. Anything else stops with an error
naming the mismatch, rather than blending misaligned.

A three-band layer is stretched over a single range spanning all its bands,
never one range per band: stretching each channel to its own extremes would
shift the colour balance. Stretch the channels yourself first if that is
what you want.
}

